{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED. Artifact 7e80c531…8bdbb386, for the causal-citation reconciliation carried by commit a0d293c277c893ed2df1903a6dc934f83fc0f34f.\n\nEvery condition holds, and I checked each by execution rather than by reading the commit.\n\nContainment, with one correction to the framing. The request names unchanged exact clean pushed main 579d7fce. Main is now 50243e70, and 579d7fce is an ancestor of it, so what I am confirming is containment rather than identity — the request's claim was true when written and is still true in the only sense that survives main moving. a0d293c2 is likewise an ancestor of current main.\n\nContent present, not merely committed. A commit can be an ancestor while a later commit reverts what it added, so I compared the current file rather than the diff: TestNamedArtifactAtTheClaimedHeadStillNeedsItsCitation exists in internal/workroom/fold_test.go in main today and is byte-identical to what a0d293c2 introduced.\n\nNo duplicate carry. The test function appears exactly once in the file, so nothing was applied twice and nothing needs applying again.\n\nThe test pins its rule, which I verified by breaking the rule rather than by watching it pass. Removing the citation requirement from resolveReviewed — trusting a named artifact at the claimed head without also requiring the report to rest on it — fails the test. That is the property worth having: it is precisely the check that stopped two of codex's approvals resolving earlier today, when they named no artifact and rested on two, and it is what keeps a reviewer's typed label from being mistaken for a followed citation.\n\nThe test is also honest about its own shape, which I want to credit. It uses t.Errorf for the head-fallback case and t.Fatalf only for the contested one, with a comment saying why: a run that loses the citation should report both losses rather than stopping at the first. That is a deliberate choice most tests get wrong by default.\n\nEvidence gates: go vet on internal/workroom is silent and the package's tests pass, including the focused pair.\n\nThe 5bf3f005 residue is obsolete. The re-review request that carried it, 763b18be, is retired and stale, and commit 5bf3f005 is not an ancestor of main — it was a superseded re-review head, so there is nothing left to act on.\n\nArchitecture: nothing moves. The change is a test in internal/workroom, adding no production surface, so the layer contract and docs/reference/architecture.md are untouched.\n\nSecurity: this strengthens rather than relaxes. The rule it pins is what prevents a verdict from claiming independence over an artifact nobody cited, which is an authority question — an approval is what gs merge acts on.\n\nSimplification: nothing to cut. Thirty-five lines pinning a rule that two live incidents have now depended on is not surplus.\n\nOne non-blocking observation, outside the conditions but worth recording where the artifact can hear it. This artifact's path is request/causal-citation-current-main-reconcile — a branch name rather than a repository path. Paths match as exact strings, so no merge at a real path can ever supersede it: it cannot be retired by ordinary succession and cannot flare when the code beneath it moves. It is the same family of defect as the artifacts at `.` and the comma-joined pseudo-paths, both of which are under their own migrations. I am not blocking on it, because the reconciliation this artifact reports is sound and the path was chosen before this was understood. It should be retired with a bare supersession by its author when that family is cleaned up, and the behaviour it describes re-anchored at internal/workroom.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7e80c53117a953e9161ff11f71315b408bdbb386","head":"579d7fce9008339138daf32d779f9e395d382d33","verdict":"approved"}}